(*
# ItemDefinition
Decodes one file of the ITEM archive (index 2 CONFIGS, archive 10) into a
`TItemDefinition`. A port of `ItemLoader.java` and the field defaults of
`ItemDefinition.java`; the Java is the specification, this docstring records
where and why the port departs from it.

## Divergences from the Java

1. **An unrecognised opcode raises.** `ItemLoader.decodeValues` logs
   `Unrecognized opcode {}` and returns, leaving the cursor parked mid-field.
   Every subsequent read then takes its bytes from the wrong offset, so one
   unknown opcode silently corrupts the rest of the definition rather than
   losing a single field. Here it raises with the opcode, the item id and the
   byte offset, which is also the fastest way to spot a cache revision that
   introduced a new opcode.
2. **The stream must be exactly consumed.** After the terminating `0` opcode the
   cursor has to sit at end-of-data. The Java never checks, but a mis-sized
   opcode is otherwise invisible on the many definitions where the damaged field
   happens to still decode: across all 33372 items in the ITEM archive not one
   leaves a trailing byte, so this is a real, always-on parity check rather than
   a heuristic.
3. **`''` stands in for Java's `null` strings.** `Name`, `Examine`, `Unknown1`,
   `InterfaceOptions[i]` and `Subops[i][j]` are `null` in the Java when unset and
   empty here, so "unset" and "set to empty" are indistinguishable. Same caveat
   as `TEntityOps.Ops` - see `entityops.simba`. A serialiser reproducing the
   Java JSON has to write `null` for these - and it cannot decide which to
   write from this record alone: **243 items in the committed
   `jsons/item-definitions.zip` ship `"examine": ""`**, a genuinely empty
   string rather than an omitted key, so both shapes occur in the ground truth.
   An earlier version of this note claimed no such item existed; the parity
   sweep in `dev/tests/320_parity_2625.simba` measured otherwise. Distinguishing
   them needs a separate "was this opcode seen" flag, which this record does
   not carry.

## Things the opcode table gets wrong if read casually

- `ColorFind`/`ColorReplace`/`TextureFind`/`TextureReplace` are read as unsigned
  shorts but **stored as signed shorts** (`(short) stream.readUnsignedShort()`),
  so anything from 32768 up comes out negative. Item 22 "Blue cog" reads 43968
  and the Java pipeline's `22.json` says `-21568`; storing the unsigned value
  would break JSON parity for the 4199 items whose palettes cross 0x8000.
- `Tradeable` defaults to **True** - opcode 15 only ever clears it. There is no
  opcode that sets it.
- `Category` and `InventoryModel` default to **0**, not -1. 22065 of the 33745
  committed item JSONs carry `"category": 0` and none carries -1.
- `Ops` is not empty by default: the client seeds ground op index 2 with "Take"
  (`ItemDefinition.java:78`), which is why every item JSON shows
  `"ops":[null,null,{"text":"Take"}]` even with no opcode 30..34 in its bytes.
- `InterfaceOptions[4]` defaults to "Drop" for the same reason.
- `Subops` (opcode 43) is a **separate field from `Ops.SubOps`**. The Java keeps
  `def.subops` and `def.groundOps` apart, and only `Ops.SubOps` is fed by opcode
  200. Opcode 43 is also the one place where a value is read and then thrown
  away: an out-of-range op or sub-op id still consumes its string.

## Post-processing this does NOT do
`SimbaItemDumper.link()` calls `linkNote`/`linkBought`/`linkPlaceholder` to copy
name, cost and members from an item's unnoted or placeholder counterpart. That
needs the whole archive, so it belongs to a later pass, not to a single-file
loader - and the committed `jsons/item-definitions.zip` is in fact *unlinked*,
matching `Load` output field-for-field.
*)
{$DEFINE WL_CACHE_DEF_ITEM_INCLUDED}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_BYTESTREAM_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE ../bytestream.simba}{$ENDIF}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_ENTITYOPS_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE entityops.simba}{$ENDIF}

const
  // The client's own allocation sizes, hardcoded in ItemLoader.decodeValues:
  // opcode 43 keeps op ids 0..4 with sub-op ids 0..19, opcodes 100..109 fill a
  // fixed 10-slot stack-size table. They are deliberately not shared with
  // entityops.simba's ENTITY_* constants - the numbers coincide, the fields do not.
  ITEM_SUBOP_ROWS: Integer = 5;
  ITEM_SUBOP_COLS: Integer = 20;
  ITEM_COUNT_SLOTS: Integer = 10;
  ITEM_INTERFACE_OPTIONS: Integer = 5;

(*
## TItemDefinition
```pascal
TItemDefinition = record
  Id: Integer;
  Name, Examine, Unknown1: String;
  ResizeX, ResizeY, ResizeZ: Integer;
  XAn2D, YAn2D, ZAn2D: Integer;
  Zoom2D, XOffset2D, YOffset2D: Integer;
  Cost, Stackable, InventoryModel: Integer;
  Tradeable, GeTradeable, Members: Boolean;
  WearPos1, WearPos2, WearPos3: Integer;
  ColorFind, ColorReplace, TextureFind, TextureReplace: TIntegerArray;
  Ambient, Contrast: Integer;
  CountObj, CountCo: TIntegerArray;
  Ops: TEntityOps;
  InterfaceOptions: TStringArray;
  Subops: array of TStringArray;
  MaleModel0, MaleModel1, MaleModel2, MaleOffset: Integer;
  MaleHeadModel, MaleHeadModel2: Integer;
  FemaleModel0, FemaleModel1, FemaleModel2, FemaleOffset: Integer;
  FemaleHeadModel, FemaleHeadModel2: Integer;
  Category, NotedId, NotedTemplate: Integer;
  Team, Weight, ShiftClickDropIndex: Integer;
  BoughtId, BoughtTemplateId: Integer;
  PlaceholderId, PlaceholderTemplateId: Integer;
  Params: TCacheParams;
end;
```
One record per item. It carries dynamic arrays and an embedded `TEntityOps`, so
copying it and then mutating the copy writes through to the original for the
array fields - the same trap `TEntityOps` documents. `Default` hands back freshly
allocated arrays on every call, so definitions produced by `Load` never alias
each other.

`CountObj`/`CountCo` are the stack-size ladder used to swap an item's inventory
model as the stack grows (coins: 996 at 2, 997 at 3, ... 1004 at 10000). They are
empty unless opcode 100..109 appeared, and 10 slots long once it did.
*)
type
  TItemDefinition = record
    Id: Integer;

    Name, Examine, Unknown1: String;

    ResizeX, ResizeY, ResizeZ: Integer;
    XAn2D, YAn2D, ZAn2D: Integer;
    Zoom2D, XOffset2D, YOffset2D: Integer;

    Cost, Stackable, InventoryModel: Integer;
    Tradeable, GeTradeable, Members: Boolean;

    WearPos1, WearPos2, WearPos3: Integer;

    // Signed 16-bit, see the docstring: the Java casts these to short.
    ColorFind, ColorReplace, TextureFind, TextureReplace: TIntegerArray;

    Ambient, Contrast: Integer;
    CountObj, CountCo: TIntegerArray;

    Ops: TEntityOps;
    InterfaceOptions: TStringArray;
    Subops: array of TStringArray;

    MaleModel0, MaleModel1, MaleModel2, MaleOffset: Integer;
    MaleHeadModel, MaleHeadModel2: Integer;
    FemaleModel0, FemaleModel1, FemaleModel2, FemaleOffset: Integer;
    FemaleHeadModel, FemaleHeadModel2: Integer;

    Category, NotedId, NotedTemplate: Integer;
    Team, Weight, ShiftClickDropIndex: Integer;
    BoughtId, BoughtTemplateId: Integer;
    PlaceholderId, PlaceholderTemplateId: Integer;

    Params: TCacheParams;
  end;

(*
## TItemDefinition.Default
```pascal
function TItemDefinition.Default(id: Integer): TItemDefinition; static;
```
The state an item starts in before a single opcode is read, mirroring the field
initialisers of `ItemDefinition.java`. Two of these defaults are observable
values rather than "unset" markers and are easy to get wrong: `Tradeable` starts
True and ground op 2 starts as "Take".
*)
function TItemDefinition.Default(id: Integer): TItemDefinition; static;
begin
  Result.Id := id;

  Result.Name := 'null';
  Result.ResizeX := 128;
  Result.ResizeY := 128;
  Result.ResizeZ := 128;
  Result.Zoom2D := 2000;
  Result.Cost := 1;
  Result.Tradeable := True;
  Result.WearPos1 := -1;
  Result.WearPos2 := -1;
  Result.WearPos3 := -1;

  Result.MaleModel0 := -1;
  Result.MaleModel1 := -1;
  Result.MaleModel2 := -1;
  Result.MaleHeadModel := -1;
  Result.MaleHeadModel2 := -1;
  Result.FemaleModel0 := -1;
  Result.FemaleModel1 := -1;
  Result.FemaleModel2 := -1;
  Result.FemaleHeadModel := -1;
  Result.FemaleHeadModel2 := -1;

  Result.NotedId := -1;
  Result.NotedTemplate := -1;
  Result.ShiftClickDropIndex := -2;
  Result.BoughtId := -1;
  Result.BoughtTemplateId := -1;
  Result.PlaceholderId := -1;
  Result.PlaceholderTemplateId := -1;

  // Everything else is 0/''/False already, which is what the Java leaves it at:
  // Category, InventoryModel, Team, Weight, Ambient, Contrast, the offsets and
  // the 2D angles included.

  Result.Ops := TEntityOps.Create();
  // The client seeds the ground menu with "Take"; opcode 32 can overwrite it,
  // nothing ever removes it.
  Result.Ops.Ops[2] := 'Take';

  SetLength(Result.InterfaceOptions, ITEM_INTERFACE_OPTIONS);
  Result.InterfaceOptions[ITEM_INTERFACE_OPTIONS - 1] := 'Drop';
end;

// Opcodes 40 and 41 share this. Java allocates both arrays at the declared
// length before filling them, so a truncated block would leave zeroes rather
// than a short array - immaterial here because the reads raise first.
procedure _ItemReadShortPairs(var s: TByteStream; var find, replace: TIntegerArray);
var
  i, n: Integer;
begin
  n := s.ReadUByte();
  SetLength(find, n);
  SetLength(replace, n);
  for i := 0 to n - 1 do
  begin
    // Read unsigned, store signed: `(short) readUnsignedShort()` in the Java.
    find[i] := s.ReadShort();
    replace[i] := s.ReadShort();
  end;
end;

// Opcode 43. `Subops` is lazily allocated, exactly as the Java lazily news
// `String[5][]` and `String[20]`, so "no opcode 43" stays distinguishable from
// "opcode 43 with no entries". Out-of-range ids are dropped but their string is
// always consumed - dropping the read too would desynchronise the stream.
procedure _ItemDecodeSubops(var s: TByteStream; var subops: array of TStringArray);
var
  opId, subId: Integer;
  valid: Boolean;
  text: String;
begin
  opId := s.ReadUByte();

  if Length(subops) = 0 then
    SetLength(subops, ITEM_SUBOP_ROWS);

  valid := (opId >= 0) and (opId < ITEM_SUBOP_ROWS);
  if valid and (Length(subops[opId]) = 0) then
    SetLength(subops[opId], ITEM_SUBOP_COLS);

  while True do
  begin
    subId := s.ReadUByte() - 1;
    if subId = -1 then Break;

    text := s.ReadJagexString();
    if valid and (subId >= 0) and (subId < ITEM_SUBOP_COLS) then
      subops[opId][subId] := text;
  end;
end;

(*
## TItemDefinition.Load
```pascal
function TItemDefinition.Load(id: Integer; data: TByteArray): TItemDefinition; static;
```
Decodes one ITEM archive file. `data` is the file's bytes as
`TCacheStore.ReadFiles` returns them; `id` is its file id, which is the item id
(and is **not** its position in that array - use `FilePositionOf`).

Raises on an unrecognised opcode and on leftover bytes after the terminating
opcode 0; both mean the reader and the cache disagree about a field width, and
both messages carry the item id and the byte offset.

```pascal
files := store.ReadFiles(2, Ord(EConfigType.ITEM), []);
item := TItemDefinition.Load(4151, files[FilePositionOf(table, Ord(EConfigType.ITEM), 4151)]);
WriteLn(item.Name);  // Abyssal whip
```
*)
function TItemDefinition.Load(id: Integer; data: TByteArray): TItemDefinition; static;
var
  s: TByteStream;
  opcode, start: Integer;
begin
  Result := TItemDefinition.Default(id);
  s := TByteStream.Create(data);

  while True do
  begin
    // Kept for the error messages: pointing at the opcode itself is far more
    // useful than pointing at wherever its operands ran out.
    start := s.Position;
    opcode := s.ReadUByte();
    if opcode = 0 then Break;

    case opcode of
      1: Result.InventoryModel := s.ReadUShort();
      2: Result.Name := s.ReadJagexString();
      3: Result.Examine := s.ReadJagexString();
      4: Result.Zoom2D := s.ReadUShort();
      5: Result.XAn2D := s.ReadUShort();
      6: Result.YAn2D := s.ReadUShort();
      // Read unsigned then folded into [-32768, 32767]; the Java spells out the
      // subtraction here instead of using readShort, but it is the same value.
      7: Result.XOffset2D := s.ReadShort();
      8: Result.YOffset2D := s.ReadShort();
      9: Result.Unknown1 := s.ReadJagexString();
      11: Result.Stackable := 1;
      12: Result.Cost := s.ReadInt();
      13: Result.WearPos1 := s.ReadByte();
      14: Result.WearPos2 := s.ReadByte();
      15: Result.Tradeable := False;
      16: Result.Members := True;
      23:
        begin
          Result.MaleModel0 := s.ReadUShort();
          Result.MaleOffset := s.ReadUByte();
        end;
      24: Result.MaleModel1 := s.ReadUShort();
      25:
        begin
          Result.FemaleModel0 := s.ReadUShort();
          Result.FemaleOffset := s.ReadUByte();
        end;
      26: Result.FemaleModel1 := s.ReadUShort();
      27: Result.WearPos3 := s.ReadByte();
      30..34: Result.Ops.DecodeOp(s, opcode - 30);
      35..39: Result.InterfaceOptions[opcode - 35] := s.ReadJagexString();
      40: _ItemReadShortPairs(s, Result.ColorFind, Result.ColorReplace);
      41: _ItemReadShortPairs(s, Result.TextureFind, Result.TextureReplace);
      42: Result.ShiftClickDropIndex := s.ReadByte();
      43: _ItemDecodeSubops(s, Result.Subops);
      44: Result.InventoryModel := s.ReadInt();
      45:
        begin
          Result.MaleModel0 := s.ReadInt();
          Result.MaleOffset := s.ReadUByte();
        end;
      46: Result.MaleModel1 := s.ReadInt();
      47: Result.MaleModel2 := s.ReadInt();
      48:
        begin
          Result.FemaleModel0 := s.ReadInt();
          Result.FemaleOffset := s.ReadUByte();
        end;
      49: Result.FemaleModel1 := s.ReadInt();
      50: Result.FemaleModel2 := s.ReadInt();
      51: Result.MaleHeadModel := s.ReadInt();
      52: Result.MaleHeadModel2 := s.ReadInt();
      53: Result.FemaleHeadModel := s.ReadInt();
      54: Result.FemaleHeadModel2 := s.ReadInt();
      65: Result.GeTradeable := True;
      75: Result.Weight := s.ReadShort();
      78: Result.MaleModel2 := s.ReadUShort();
      79: Result.FemaleModel2 := s.ReadUShort();
      90: Result.MaleHeadModel := s.ReadUShort();
      91: Result.FemaleHeadModel := s.ReadUShort();
      92: Result.MaleHeadModel2 := s.ReadUShort();
      93: Result.FemaleHeadModel2 := s.ReadUShort();
      94: Result.Category := s.ReadUShort();
      95: Result.ZAn2D := s.ReadUShort();
      97: Result.NotedId := s.ReadUShort();
      98: Result.NotedTemplate := s.ReadUShort();
      100..109:
        begin
          if Length(Result.CountObj) = 0 then
          begin
            SetLength(Result.CountObj, ITEM_COUNT_SLOTS);
            SetLength(Result.CountCo, ITEM_COUNT_SLOTS);
          end;
          Result.CountObj[opcode - 100] := s.ReadUShort();
          Result.CountCo[opcode - 100] := s.ReadUShort();
        end;
      110: Result.ResizeX := s.ReadUShort();
      111: Result.ResizeY := s.ReadUShort();
      112: Result.ResizeZ := s.ReadUShort();
      113: Result.Ambient := s.ReadByte();
      // Stored RAW, as the cache holds it and as RuneLite's ItemLoader reads
      // it - which is what the committed item-definitions.zip contains, so
      // scaling here would cost 934 items of JSON parity.
      //
      // The renderer MUST scale it by 5 before use. The decompiled client
      // scales at decode (LostCityRS/OS1 ObjType.java:311,
      // `this.contrast = buf.g1b() * 5`) and consumes it as
      // `light(ambient + 64, contrast + 768, ...)`. RuneLite omits that scale
      // in BOTH its loader and its renderer, so its own item sprites are
      // wrong: measured over 3331 rendered items, raw gives 3158 matches
      // against the committed hashes and x5 gives 3331. See
      // docs/phase2-render-spec.md.
      114: Result.Contrast := s.ReadByte();
      115: Result.Team := s.ReadUByte();
      139: Result.BoughtId := s.ReadUShort();
      140: Result.BoughtTemplateId := s.ReadUShort();
      148: Result.PlaceholderId := s.ReadUShort();
      149: Result.PlaceholderTemplateId := s.ReadUShort();
      160: Result.Stackable := 2;
      200: Result.Ops.DecodeSubOp(s);
      201: Result.Ops.DecodeConditionalOp(s);
      202: Result.Ops.DecodeConditionalSubOp(s);
      249: Result.Params := s.ReadParams();
      else
        raise 'ItemDefinition: unknown opcode ' + ToStr(opcode) + ' in item ' +
              ToStr(id) + ' at offset ' + ToStr(start);
    end;
  end;

  if s.Position <> s.Length() then
    raise 'ItemDefinition: item ' + ToStr(id) + ' ended at offset ' +
          ToStr(s.Position) + ' with ' + ToStr(s.Remaining()) +
          ' bytes left of ' + ToStr(s.Length());

  // ItemLoader.post: a stackable item carries no weight, whatever opcode 75 said.
  if Result.Stackable = 1 then
    Result.Weight := 0;
end;
